<!DOCTYPE HTML PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD HTML 4.01//EN"
        "http://www.w3.org/TR/html4/strict.dtd">

<html>
  <head>
    <title>Machiavelli - Order (and re-order) cards til you have no more</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Documentation of the Machiavelli cardgame">
    <meta name="keywords" content="Documentation, Docu, Machiavelli, game, card, cardgame">

    <meta name="DC.Creator" content="Markus Schwab">
    <meta name="DC.Date" content="2003-11-30">
    <meta name="DC.Rights" content="Copyright (C) 2003 - 2006, distributed under the GNU Free Documentation License">
  </head>

  <body>
    <h1 align="center">Machiavelli</h1>
    <hr size=2>

    <p>I learned Machiavelli from a swiss guy, who itself learned it from
      italiens (which in turn might have learned it from someone else). And
      actually I'm not even sure, if the name is right ...</p>

    <p>Anyway - the rules:</p>

    <p>It's all about putting down "matching" cards and that way getting rid
      of its cards. "Matching" means that either the numbers must be equal
      (independend of the colour, but each colour only once) or the colour and
      the numbers are in a sequence (Ace (as one), two, three, ..., ten,
      jack, queen, king, ace).</p>

    <p>Every pile on the table must contain at least 3 cards.</p>

    <p>Every player starts with 7 cards in his hand and takes another one at
      the beginning of its turn from the pile of reserve (this happens
      automatically by the computer). Then he can either put his cards to
      existing piles, re-sort them however he likes, build new piles (even
      with cards from existing piles) - in a nutshell doing as he likes, as
      long as at the end of its turn all piles follow the rules above.</p>

    <p>The cards are played and/or sorted with drag and drop<a
      href="Machiavelli.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>.

    <p>If the player can't or won't continue, continues the next player still
      having cards (counter-clockwise). This end of the turn is indicated by clicking
      in the pile of the reserve. If there are piles in an invalid condition
      appears a window with an error message. This window also allows an undo
      of the last or all actions in the actual turn.</p>

    <p>The last player still having cards has lost.</p>

    <p>The status line gives some information about what's going on!</p>

    <h2>Note to the layout</h2>
    <p>After starting a game you have (from left to right and from top to
      bottom):</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The pile of reserve: A heap of cards (with face down), from where
        you put up new cards (if you put the mouse over it you are informed
        about the remaining cards).</li>

      <li>The cards of the player. Presorted by colour, but re-sortable with
        drag and drop<a href="Machiavelli.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a>.</li>

      <li>The piles on the table. Cards can be added to that piles
        with drag und drop<a href="Buraco.html.en#Note1"><sup>1</sup></a> (if
        it is the turn of the player). Note that both cards from the hand of
        the player as cards already on the table can be moved.</li>

      <li>In the last row are the cards of the other (computer) player.</li>
     </ul>

    <hr size=1 noshade>
    <p style="text-indent:-0.3cm;margin-left:0.3cm"><a name="Note1"></a>1)
      Drag and drop means clicking with the a mouse button on the card,
      holding the button and then dragging the card to its destination.
      The end of this activity is signaled by releasing the button.</p>

